WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.730
By now you're probably
familiar with querying

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.280
values or records
within database tables.

3
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.530
But how do you query
specific records and

4
00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.260
values if they're duplicated
across the table.

5
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.630
When you come across
obstacles like these,

6
00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.985
you can use keys
as your solution.

7
00:00:14.985 --> 00:00:17.160
In this video,
you'll learn how to

8
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.020
explain the purpose
of primary key

9
00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.820
in a database table and select

10
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.025
a simple and composite
primary key.

11
00:00:23.025 --> 00:00:25.020
You may have encountered
several examples

12
00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.820
of primary keys
during this course.

13
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.090
In these examples, you saw
that they're using tables as

14
00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.590
unique method to identify

15
00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.810
a record and prevent duplicates.

16
00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.820
Let's take an example
of a student table

17
00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.170
with five attributes,

18
00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.330
ID, name, date of birth,

19
00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:42.495
email, and grade.

20
00:00:42.495 --> 00:00:44.090
How could we identify

21
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.610
a specific student to
enter their grade?

22
00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.370
The student Mary, on row 2.

23
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.890
All you need to do is
find the unique ID

24
00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.475
of Mary to identify a
record of her data.

25
00:00:54.475 --> 00:00:56.165
However, in this example,

26
00:00:56.165 --> 00:00:58.175
you can't use the
student name column

27
00:00:58.175 --> 00:01:01.055
because there are two students
in the table called Mary.

28
00:01:01.055 --> 00:01:03.320
You can't use the date
of birth either because

29
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:04.520
another student in a table

30
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:06.605
called Dan has the
same birthday.

31
00:01:06.605 --> 00:01:09.165
Neither of these records
are unique to Mary,

32
00:01:09.165 --> 00:01:11.210
so what's the best approach?

33
00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:14.495
The solution is to
locate a candidate key.

34
00:01:14.495 --> 00:01:17.000
This is an attribute that's
unique to each row of

35
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.775
the table and I cannot
have a null value.

36
00:01:19.775 --> 00:01:22.475
In other words, it
cannot be empty.

37
00:01:22.475 --> 00:01:26.300
In this example, there are
two possible candidate keys,

38
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.925
the student ID and
the student email.

39
00:01:28.925 --> 00:01:32.210
Both rows contain a unique
value for each student,

40
00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.635
so either one can be
used as a primary key.

41
00:01:35.635 --> 00:01:39.170
Let's assign the student
ID as the primary key.

42
00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.150
Whichever column you reject as

43
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.795
the primary key becomes the
alternate or secondary key.

44
00:01:44.795 --> 00:01:48.955
In this instance, the email
column is a secondary key.

45
00:01:48.955 --> 00:01:51.260
But what happens if
you can't locate

46
00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.350
a unique value within the table?

47
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.770
Maybe all rows of
duplicated values.

48
00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:00.725
In this instance, you can
create a composite primary key,

49
00:02:00.725 --> 00:02:02.869
this type of key
in the combination

50
00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:04.900
of two or more attributes.

51
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.295
Let's take the example of
the delivery department

52
00:02:07.295 --> 00:02:08.735
of an online store.

53
00:02:08.735 --> 00:02:10.610
They have a delivery
table, the tracks and

54
00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.965
the deliveries placed
by their customers.

55
00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:14.690
However, there's
no single column

56
00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:16.930
with unique values in each row.

57
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:20.000
No column can be considered
as the primary key.

58
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.300
In this case, the best
approach is to combine

59
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.880
the customer ID and
project code columns to

60
00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:29.170
create a unique value for
each specific record of data.

61
00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.340
With these columns,
you can determine

62
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.580
which customer ordered
what product so together,

63
00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:37.805
these columns become the
composite primary key.

64
00:02:37.805 --> 00:02:39.620
This key can be used to track

65
00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.780
the delivery status
for each customer.

66
00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:44.030
You're now familiar
with one single column

67
00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.530
primary and composite
primary key.

68
00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.680
You should now also
be able to identify

69
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.690
the most appropriate
situation in which to

70
00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.940
use each one. Great work.